---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { timeline } from '../data/timeline';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
---
<Base title="About" description="About Haibin Meng: education, internship, honours, hobbies and a timeline from 2018.">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lead">I'm Haibin Meng (孟海彬), MayHappy online since 2019. I measure tiny motions with radar, and in my spare time I build small machines and let coding agents help me build more of them.</p>
  <p>I like finding the core question behind a phenomenon, modelling it, designing a method, and an experiment to check it. When something breaks, I go back and model it again until it is solved. I can never lose my interest in learning: when I first learned that a radar's angular resolution depends on its aperture, my first thought was to move the radar to make the aperture bigger, and only later found that this idea has a name, synthetic aperture.</p>
  <p>Say hi: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a></p>

  <h2 data-under>Education</h2>
  <ul class="list">
    <li data-icon="cap">
      <span class="title">Shanghai Jiao Tong University, 2022 – (expected 2027)</span>
      Combined master–PhD track, School of Mechanical Engineering, State Key Laboratory of Mechanical System and Vibration. Advisor Prof. Zhike Peng. PhD proposal defended October 2025.
    </li>
    <li data-icon="cap">
      <span class="title">Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen, 2018 – 2022</span>
      B.Eng. in Mechanical Design, Manufacturing and Automation, ranked 2nd of 74. Thesis on whole-body planning for a quadruped with an arm, advised by Prof. Wenfu Xu. Undergraduate research with Prof. Honghai Liu and Dr. Han Yuan.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 data-under id="internship">Internship</h2>
  <ul class="list">
    <li data-icon="work">
      <span class="title">Intern, AI video agent team, bilibili (花生), Sep 2026 – present</span>
      <p style="margin-top:6px">In September 2026 I joined the team behind 花生, an AI video-creation agent: you give it a sentence or a script, and an LLM agent plans the storyboard, gathers footage, adds a voice-over and assembles a video. It was my first internship, and my background is microwave-radar vibration measurement, not web software.</p>
      <p>My lead's brief was unusual: not an engineer who waits for tickets, but a "vertical" engineer, someone who understands the users, the product and the technical detail well enough to find problems that are real and solvable today. When I proposed post-training a model because it sounded exciting, my lead called it holding a hammer and looking for nails. Problem first, technique second.</p>
      <p>So I started from the ground. I used the product as a creator and wrote down where it let me down. I mapped the codebase with coding agents, and when their overviews did not give me real understanding, I worked through the agent runtime piece by piece, learning TypeScript, asynchronous programming and streaming on the way, and wrote two long beginner's guides for myself.</p>
      <p>Then came real bugs. In one, the agent occasionally got stuck calling the same read-only tool over and over. Fresh test runs never showed it, so I built a replay setup that resumes a session from the exact state where a real run had gone wrong. That showed the loop feeds on itself: once a few identical turns are in the context, the model tends to keep copying them. The fix was a small guard that declines an identical repeated call and tells the model to move on, without ending the user's turn. I measured it against unguarded runs before it was merged.</p>
      <p>Another investigation, into numbers the agent occasionally misreported, ended differently: hundreds of replays could not reproduce it, and we decided it was rare enough to leave alone. A third task was bringing a safeguard from an older version of the agent to the new one: when the model says it will call a tool but doesn't, the run is nudged to actually act. I checked it against past runs for false alarms, and after release I watched live traces until it fired and confirmed the runs recovered.</p>
      <p>What I took away:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>What made me productive in radar research carries over: know the real problems, and know the principle in full detail, until the system runs in your head.</li>
        <li>With LLM agents, rare failures are normal. Reproducing them from the real context, with enough runs, beats arguing from a single trace.</li>
        <li>Coding agents wrote much of my code. My job was to understand every line I shipped and explain it to a reviewer.</li>
        <li>A short, evidence-first notebook (problems, tasks, a five-line diary) helped more than any long log.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

